on the afternoon of 20th november 1952  a small group of people milled around a  canyon  outside desert center california  led by the self-style professor george  adamski  the group came to this isolated spot  halfway between  palm springs and the arizona border to  photograph  flying saucers among them was  25 year old george hunt williamson  an ex-military man an amateur  archaeologist  61 year old professor adamski set up a  telescope  and waited for the alien craft to appear  and he wasn't disappointed he claimed  not only to see  various spacecraft but also to meet a  real  live venusian named orthot  only adamski witnessed the visitation  but his young helper williamson took  plaster casts  of desert footprints that revealed  cryptic symbols on the soles of his  shoes  adamski recounted the experience in his  book  flying saucers have landed and he became  a leading figure  in the so-called golden age of ufos  incidentally 1952 was also the year that  the air force started  its longest and most serious study of  the ufo phenomenon  project blue book the most interesting  and mysterious person at desert center  however was  george hunt williamson he was no  run-of-the-mill nut in a tinfoil hat  maybe the first thing to understand  about george hunt williamson  is that he was born in chicago under the  name of george  leonard williamson and during his  lifetime he would assume  various identities drafted into the u.s  army air corps in 1945  williamson was assigned to public  relations  and coincidentally or not air force pr  personnel later played prominent roles  in investigating and debunking  ufo sightings after leaving the military  williamson relocated to arizona to  attend the university of arizona  in pursuit of a graduate degree in  archaeology  that same year he also traveled to  lincoln county new mexico  about an hour west of roswell to work on  an archaeological dig  or so he claimed only two years earlier  roswell had been the site of probably  the first and most controversial  reported  ufo crash so was williamson hunting  for indian artifacts or something else  i should add that williamson was briefly  recalled to u.s military service in 1949  and received a second lieutenant's  commission  later he also received a commission in  the newly christened air force's  civilian auxiliary the civil air patrol  becoming a lieutenant colonel  williamson's academic studies came to a  halt in 1951  when he was expelled from the university  for unspecified reasons  in the meantime he picked up a wife  betty  who became his enthusiastic assistant  professor george adamski meanwhile  first arrived in southern california  during the 1920s  when a hoped-for movie career didn't pan  out he opted to form  a cult this was the royal order of tibet  also known as the  temple of scientific philosophy  really it was a thinly disguised rip-off  of an  older mystical school called the  theosophical society  that blended eastern and western  mysticism  and pioneered what we now call the new  age movement  adamski dispensed timeless wisdom for a  modest fee  but the real secret of a success was the  religious  exemption that the royal order of tibet  received to make  and serve wine at its gatherings after  all  these were america's prohibition years  repealed in december 1933 spelled doom  for adamski's booze dispensing temple of  scientific philosophy  so by 1940 he was flipping burgers at a  little roadside cafe  at the foot of mount palomar adamski had  no connection to the observatory there  but he tried hard to give the impression  that he did he set up a couple of  telescopes and gave lectures to gullible  tourists  when flying saucers became the rage  adamski started snapping photographs  of mysterious airborne objects  that attracted a small band of followers  including  george hunt williamson still i wonder if  it was  mere curiosity that brought williamson  to desert center  that day in 1952  maybe the army air corps veteran was  there on someone  else's orders to check out adamski and  his group  in any event williamson went on to  publish his own book  the saucer speak and its sequel other  tongues  other flesh around this time williamson  also showed up on the radar of fbi  director j edgar hoover  the fbi was concerned that some of the  alien visitors reported by ufo  enthusiasts  preached universal brotherhood and  nuclear disarmament  it raised suspicions that this flying  saucer business  might be an insidious commie plot  the fbi in investigating williamson  soon turned up evidence that he was  involved in smuggling antiquities  and maybe drugs out of mexico still  the feds never touched him and maybe  that's related to those  national security redactions in his file  not long afterward williamson gathered  up betty and headed off to peru  there calling himself brother philip he  claimed to represent a secret order  called the brotherhood  of the seven rays was this a genuine  spiritual quest  or more cover if running an esoteric  monastery in the andes  was what williamson was really up to it  proved difficult  in 1958 betty died of fever and  malnutrition  though rumors circulated that she'd been  pushed off a cliff  soon after williamson started calling  himself michelle dobrnovich  now in addition to claiming to be the  duke of sumatra  he also purported to be the heir to the  throne of serbia  and that wasn't the half of it  williamson  i mean dobrnovich joined a bogus  offshoot of the knights of malta  headquartered in shikshini pennsylvania  its ranks were filled with aging  fascists and  retired military and intelligence  officers  a few years later in the early 1970s  dobrnovich was consecrated as a bishop  in the obscure  american orthodox catholic church  an outfit with more links to intelligent  circles  and he relocated to santa barbara  california  there he founded his own religious order  called  the holy apostolic catholic church  and he briefly married a former b movie  actress  at the time i was a history graduate  student  at uc santa barbara and once met the  former  george hunt williamson i don't recall  much about the encounter  but the person who introduced us was a  retired  air force colonel of all things standing  back and looking at the strange career  of george hunt williamson i can't help  but suspect that he was a spy of some  sort  the only question is whose  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

